## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Establish a separate development site where Tool Shed website and dashboard changes can be
deployed, exercised, and refined without changing the public `ts.rookaro.com` site. The selected
topology keeps production and development on `sup`, with development in a separately isolated
Docker Compose stack and separate host filesystem tree, reachable only from the LAN or through a
Workpc localhost forward. Required restart independence is limited to development web containers
and services, so a separate host or VM is not needed. The current project configuration is
explicitly combined; this idea moves toward a deliberate split development/production workflow
without adding a second physical server.

### Why It Matters

Tool Shed now has a hosted dashboard, persistent data, authentication, reporter contracts, and
frequent site releases. Iterating directly against the public site increases the chance that an
unfinished UI, incompatible schema, broken migration, or diagnostic change becomes externally
visible before it is ready. A development site should make realistic validation routine while
keeping public promotion intentional.

### Desired Outcome

- Routine site and dashboard development happens on a clearly identified non-production target.
- The development environment is close enough to production to expose deployment, migration,
  authentication, browser, and reporter-integration defects.
- Development cannot accidentally mutate the production database, credentials, enrollment state,
  reports, or public endpoint.
- Promotion to `ts.rookaro.com` is a separate, explicit, evidence-backed action with a practical
  rollback path.
- The project configuration and operator documentation accurately describe the split model.
- The Linux and Windows test beds reach development directly over the LAN, while Workpc reaches it
  privately through the existing VS Code/SSH path to `sup`.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Known constraint: the public site must remain available and authoritative while the development
  environment is introduced.
- Known constraint: production secrets and live reporter credentials must not be copied into the
  development environment.
- Approved data boundary: development starts with an empty database plus deterministic synthetic
  seed data. Production credentials, reporter enrollments, sessions, and personal data must never
  be cloned into development.
- Known constraint: environment identity must be obvious in the UI, URLs, configuration, logs, and
  deployment commands so an operator cannot casually confuse the targets.
- Working preference: reuse the existing application and deployment machinery where practical
  instead of maintaining a divergent second stack.
- Selected host boundary: production and development share `sup` (`192.168.7.5`) but use separate
  host filesystem trees, Compose project identity, containers, ports, networks, databases, Docker
  volumes, secrets, logs, and backup/restore paths. No writable bind mount is shared between the
  environments. Development service/container restarts must target only the development Compose
  project; host and Docker-daemon restart independence is not required.
- Verified production boundary: production already runs as Compose project `tsrookarocom` from
  `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com`, with its own three-service stack, default network, PostgreSQL
  and static-data volumes, and published host port `8087`. It is Compose-isolated but shares the
  host Docker daemon with other stacks.
- Selected network boundary: development is LAN-only and must not be registered with the public
  Nginx Proxy Manager on `iotmaster` or the public Rookaro hostname router.
- Confirmed clients: the Linux test bed runs on `sup`; the Windows test bed runs on `gogetter`
  (`192.168.7.113`); Workpc is outside the LAN and runs the VS Code desktop connected remotely to
  `sup`.
- Reporter constraint: one workspace identity has one saved dashboard connection. Pointing an
  existing workspace at development replaces that workspace's saved production dashboard
  destination; development reporting therefore needs disposable development-only workspace
  identities or an explicit reassignment.
- Explicit non-goal: this brainstorming turn does not provision infrastructure, alter DNS, deploy
  a site, migrate data, or change the current public release process.
- Explicit non-goal: the development site does not become a second source of Tool Shed project
  truth or a remote-control surface for enrolled workspaces.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Stable LAN-only development stack on `sup` | Reuses the existing server, gives both test beds realistic access, supports Workpc forwarding, and permits dev-only container/service restarts | Host reboot, Docker-daemon restart, disk/resource exhaustion, or network/firewall changes can still interrupt production; demands strict logical isolation and scoped commands | Selected; required restart independence is at the application-stack level and `8443` LAN connectivity on `sup` is proven |
| Dedicated development VM on a shared physical host | Separate OS, Docker daemon, firewall, storage allocation, and service restarts with modest hardware cost | A physical-host reboot or failure still affects both; requires a suitable hypervisor and reserved capacity | Good middle option only if it is not hosted inside the production OS and shared-host failure is acceptable |
| Dedicated development machine or VM on a different physical host | Dev reboots, Docker changes, resource pressure, storage faults, and most maintenance are independent of production | Adds another OS lifecycle, LAN address, patching, monitoring, and power/resource cost | Not selected because host-level restart independence is not expected; retain as an escalation if that requirement changes |
| Local-only site | Lowest exposure and cost, fast iteration, production data naturally isolated | Windows test bed could not reach it directly and it would be less representative of LAN integration | Not selected as the complete solution |
| Per-branch or per-change preview environments | Excellent change isolation and reviewability | Highest deployment, cleanup, secret, database, and cost complexity | Possible later enhancement; likely more machinery than the immediate need requires |

### Verified Production And Development Model

| Boundary | Production now | Development model |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Host and Docker daemon | `sup.local`; shared host daemon | Same `sup.local` daemon |
| Filesystem root | `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com` | A different host filesystem root; exact path selected during implementation |
| Compose project | `tsrookarocom` | Distinct project, working name `tsrookarocom-dev` |
| Services | Nginx/docs, Django dashboard, PostgreSQL | Independent development instances of the same three services |
| Network | `tsrookarocom_default` | Separately generated development network |
| Persistent volumes | `tsrookarocom_dashboard_postgres` and `tsrookarocom_dashboard_static` | Separately prefixed development volumes; no production volume reuse |
| Host endpoint | Production Nginx on `8087` behind the existing public route | Plain HTTP on LAN port `8443`, with no public route |
| Bind mounts and configuration | Production `public/` and `nginx.conf` are mounted read-only; protected `.env` is production-specific | Separate generated site, Nginx configuration, and protected development environment file |
| Lifecycle commands | Scoped to `tsrookarocom` | Scoped only to the development project; dev rebuild/restart must not address production |

### Recommended Initial Integration Policy

| Integration / side effect | Initial policy | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Maintainer login and MFA | Enable with a development-only account, password, TOTP seed, and session secret | Authentication and MFA are promotion gates, but plain HTTP means no production credential may be reused |
| Database migrations, retention, and dashboard views/SSE | Enable against the isolated development database | These are core application behaviors and do not contact an external system |
| Reporter enrollment, credential issuance, and report ingestion | Enable only for disposable development-specific Linux and Windows test-bed workspace identities | Exercises the complete reporter contract without redirecting a live workspace or copying a production credential |
| App Server and work-efficiency telemetry | Allow only as bounded fields in reports from those development reporters | The dashboard displays aggregates but cannot start App Server work or mutate a workspace |
| Reporter safety scheduler and persistent worker | Start disabled; prove manual enrollment and `report now`, then enable only on the disposable development reporters | Avoids periodic traffic and saved-destination mistakes while the environment is first qualified |
| Email, webhooks, and hosted completion notifications | Keep disabled/absent | The current dashboard sends no email or webhooks; adding outbound delivery would create unrelated risk and scope |
| Production reporters, credentials, data, and scheduled jobs | Never connect or copy them into development | Preserves the approved isolation boundary and prevents misleading production state |

## Open Questions

- Should the recommended initial integration policy be accepted as written, including dedicated
  development-only test-bed workspace identities, or does any integration need different behavior?

## Decisions

- A separate development site is needed before continuing routine public-site development.
- The public site is the promotion target, not the place for ordinary iteration.
- Development will run on the production server `sup` at `192.168.7.5` in a separately named and
  isolated Docker Compose project with its own host filesystem tree. Dev container and web-service
  restarts are in scope; host reboots and Docker-daemon restarts are not part of the expected
  development workflow.
- Production's current Docker environment is the `tsrookarocom` Compose project rooted at
  `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com`. Development will mirror its Nginx/docs, Django dashboard, and
  PostgreSQL service shape under a distinct Compose project and filesystem root.
- Development will remain LAN-only. It will not receive a public Nginx Proxy Manager or Rookaro
  router entry; production remains publicly available through its existing route without change.
- The Linux test bed on `sup` and Windows test bed on `gogetter` will access the development site
  over the LAN.
- Workpc will have persistent access through an automatically started, automatically restarted
  localhost-only SSH tunnel into `sup`; access must not depend on an active VS Code session.
- The development endpoint will use plain HTTP on port `8443`, with LAN clients using
  `http://192.168.7.5:8443` and Workpc using a localhost-only forwarded endpoint. Development will
  not use TLS/SSL, public DNS, Nginx Proxy Manager, or the public Rookaro hostname router.
- Development will begin with an empty database and deterministic synthetic seed data. Production
  credentials, reporter enrollments, sessions, and personal data will not be cloned.
- Development will carry unmistakable visual and machine-readable environment identity, including
  distinct UI banners, database names, Compose names, and environment markers.
- Initial deployment will be manual from a dedicated development branch or an exact frozen commit;
  automatic deployment on every push is deferred until the environment contract is proven.
- Promotion will use the exact commit tested in development, rather than rebuilding or selecting
  different source for production.
- Promotion requires evidence that the development stack deploys and becomes healthy; migrations
  and recovery work on non-production data; login, MFA, dashboard pages, and reporter enrollment
  work; both test beds connect; Workpc forwarding works; development is unreachable through public
  routing; and production remains healthy and unchanged.
- Development will use separate volumes, database, networks, secrets, logs, and backups, with CPU
  and memory limits. If the shared-host option is retained, those limits also protect production.
- Production and development will use distinct deployment/check-out roots on `sup`, distinct image
  tags, and no shared writable bind mounts. The exact filesystem paths will be chosen during
  implementation.
- Development must not reuse production's fixed `ts-rookaro-com` container name, production network,
  production volumes, `.env`, generated `public/` directory, or Nginx configuration.
- The operating contract will include development rollback and database-reset procedures. Before
  any eventual promotion involving migrations, production will be backed up.
- The live integration/side-effect policy has not been selected.

## Don't Forget

- Give the development environment a persistent visual banner and machine-readable environment
  marker.
- Ensure filesystem separation includes deployment files, environment files, generated public
  assets, database storage, logs, and backup destinations—not only different Compose filenames.
- Use separate databases, volumes, secrets, enrollment credentials, cookies/session scope, and
  backup/restore paths.
- Prevent development reporters from being mistaken for production reporters and prevent existing
  production clients from silently reporting to development.
- Exercise database migrations and rollback/recovery using non-production data before promotion.
- Consider resource contention, monitoring, retention, backups, and cleanup—not just the
  application container.
- Update `work/tool-shed.yaml` from `combined` to `split` only after the development target and its
  operating contract are real and verified.
- Bind any Workpc-side forwarded port to `127.0.0.1`, not a public or all-interface address.
- Manage the Workpc tunnel with host-local startup and restart-on-failure behavior, SSH keepalives,
  and fail-fast port-binding checks; do not store a private key in the repository.
- Avoid a public `*.rookaro.com` development hostname because the existing wildcard DNS and TLS
  entry path could expose the environment accidentally.
- Configure development-specific allowed hosts, CSRF origins, cookie scope, and forwarded-protocol
  handling for plain-HTTP LAN access and Workpc localhost forwarding.
- Add a development-only Django security profile that permits plain HTTP without weakening the
  production HTTPS redirect, secure-cookie, proxy-header, or HSTS settings.

