  report ingestion, and optional reporter schedulers. Each workspace identity stores only one
  dashboard destination, so disposable development-only identities are recommended.
- The owner reopened the same-server decision after observing that restarting development
  infrastructure should not interrupt production. Separate Compose projects isolate ordinary
  container restarts, but a host or Docker-daemon restart and shared resource/network failures do
  not. A dedicated machine or a VM on different physical infrastructure is the recommended option
  when that independence is required.
- The owner clarified that expected restarts are limited to development web containers and
  services, not the host or Docker daemon. The shared `sup` topology is therefore selected again,
  with all restart and deployment commands required to scope explicitly to the development Compose
  project so production containers remain untouched.
- The owner further specified one Docker host on `sup.local` with separate production and
  development filesystem trees. Development runs as its own Docker Compose project; filesystem,
  volume, image-tag, environment-file, and writable-mount identity must not overlap production.
- Runtime inspection confirmed that production is already Compose-isolated as project
  `tsrookarocom` at `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com`. Its healthy Nginx/docs, Django dashboard, and
  PostgreSQL containers share `tsrookarocom_default`; its database and static data use separately
  named volumes; and only Nginx publishes host port `8087`. Production shares the host Docker daemon,
  so development will mirror this shape under a different Compose project rather than creating a
  second daemon or VM.

